## Proof

The conditions $\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} x^k p(x)\,dx = 0$ for $0 \le k \le 6$ state that $p$ is orthogonal (with respect to the inner product $\langle f,g\rangle = \int_{-\pi}^{\pi} f(x)g(x)\,dx$) to every polynomial of degree $\le 6$.

Since $p$ has degree exactly $7$, it lies in the one-dimensional orthogonal complement of $\{q : \deg q \le 6\}$ inside $\{q : \deg q \le 7\}$. This complement is spanned by the degree-$7$ orthogonal polynomial on $[-\pi,\pi]$.

**Rescaling to Legendre polynomials.** Substituting $x = \pi t$ gives
$$\int_{-\pi}^{\pi} f(x)g(x)\,dx = \pi\int_{-1}^{1} f(\pi t)\,g(\pi t)\,dt,$$
so orthogonality on $[-\pi,\pi]$ is equivalent to orthogonality on $[-1,1]$ after rescaling. Hence the degree-$7$ orthogonal polynomial on $[-\pi,\pi]$ is proportional to $P_7(x/\pi)$, where $P_7$ is the degree-$7$ Legendre polynomial:
$$P_7(t) = \tfrac{1}{16}\bigl(231\,t^7 - 315\,t^5 + 105\,t^3 - 9\,t\bigr).$$

Therefore $p(x) = c\,P_7(x/\pi)$ for some real constant $c$.

**Evaluating $P_7$ at key points.** $P_7$ is an odd function (only odd powers appear), so:
- $P_7(0) = 0$,
- $P_7(-1) = -P_7(1)$.

Computing $P_7(1)$:
$$P_7(1) = \tfrac{1}{16}(231 - 315 + 105 - 9) = \tfrac{1}{16}(12) = \tfrac{3}{4}.$$

**Determining $c$.** From $p(\pi) = \sqrt{3}$:
$$c\,P_7(1) = c\cdot\tfrac{3}{4} = \sqrt{3} \implies c = \tfrac{4\sqrt{3}}{3}.$$

**Computing the answers.**
$$p(-\pi) = c\,P_7(-1) = \tfrac{4\sqrt{3}}{3}\cdot\bigl(-\tfrac{3}{4}\bigr) = -\sqrt{3},$$
$$p(0) = c\,P_7(0) = 0.$$

Thus
$$\boxed{(p(-\pi),\, p(0)) = (-\sqrt{3},\; 0)}.$$

